+Here $I\subset\mathbb R$ is an open interval, $\lambda$ is its real coordinate, and $x'$ denotes $dx/d\lambda$. The future-timelike condition makes $x'$ nowhere zero. On a neighborhood of $[a,b]$, $x$ is $C^2$ (so its tangent is $C^1$). If $\widetilde\lambda=f(\lambda)$ is an increasing $C^1$ reparametrization with $f'>0$, then $\sqrt{-g(dx/d\widetilde\lambda,dx/d\widetilde\lambda)}\,d\widetilde\lambda=\sqrt{-g(x',x')}\,d\lambda$ by the chain rule. An admissible variation is a one-parameter family $x_\epsilon(\lambda)$ that is $C^2$ jointly in $(\epsilon,\lambda)$, has fixed endpoint events $x_\epsilon(a)=x(a)$ and $x_\epsilon(b)=x(b)$, and remains future timelike for sufficiently small $|\epsilon|$. The physical free trajectory is required to satisfy $\left.\frac{d}{d\epsilon}S_{m,[a,b]}[x_\epsilon]\right|_{\epsilon=0}=0$ for every such admissible variation and every compact segment $[a,b]\subset I$ with $a<b$. The integrand is the proper-time length density, so $S_{m,[a,b]}=-mc^2\int_a^b d\tau$; its dimension is action (J s). The adopted premise concerns this ideal free test body only. It is neither a theorem about all matter nor an empirical report, and supplies no backreaction or completeness claim. The flat ideal-clock specialization agrees with [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]]; adopting this action on a curved background remains a separate premise.
 
 The cotangent phase-space carrier convention assumes countable choice ACω only; full choice is not assumed.
